Phase 0 — Verify what we already have

Before changing anything, let's take inventory.

As root:

xbps-query -l | grep -E 'dbus|wpa_supplicant|dhcpcd|NetworkManager|seatd|turnstile'

Then:

sv status dbus
sv status wpa_supplicant
sv status dhcpcd
sv status NetworkManager
sv status seatd
sv status turnstiled

Some of those may simply say the service doesn't exist. That's fine.

Also:

lsblk -f
lsblk --discard
cat /etc/fstab

And:

uname -a
cat /etc/os-release

This gives us a clean snapshot before we modify things.

Phase 1 — Update the base system

This should always be first.

xbps-install -S
xbps-install -Su

Void's installation documentation explicitly recommends doing a system update immediately after the first boot.

Then:

xbps-install -Su

until there's nothing left to update.

Phase 2 — D-Bus

Yes, we should install D-Bus now.

Not because Mango itself necessarily needs us to build our whole system around D-Bus, but because it becomes useful/necessary for several pieces we're going to use later.

Install:

doas xbps-install -S dbus

Enable it:

doas ln -s /etc/sv/dbus /var/service/

Check:

sv status dbus

You should get something like:

run: dbus: (pid ...) ...

NetworkManager requires D-Bus and will fail without the system D-Bus service.

PipeWire also uses D-Bus for some system functionality, and Void specifically lists a missing system D-Bus service as a common PipeWire error.

So this is one of the pieces I'd establish early.

Phase 3 — NetworkManager decision

Here's where I change my earlier recommendation slightly.

You said:

"I used wpa_supplicant as I found it easier."

That's completely reasonable.

And there's an important distinction:

wpa_supplicant

Handles:

Wi-Fi authentication
       ↓
Wi-Fi association

It isn't really a complete network manager.

NetworkManager

Handles:

Wi-Fi
Ethernet
DHCP
DNS
routes
VPN
connection profiles
roaming
network state

and can use wpa_supplicant underneath.

So the architecture becomes:

             NetworkManager
                  │
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          │                │
       Ethernet       Wi-Fi management
                           │
                    wpa_supplicant

The key is NetworkManager owns the interface, not an independently running wpa_supplicant service.

Void explicitly warns that independently running wpa_supplicant alongside NetworkManager can interfere with it.

Therefore:

I recommend eventually switching to:

NetworkManager
      +
wpa_supplicant as its Wi-Fi backend

rather than:

wpa_supplicant service
      +
some separate DHCP mechanism

That will also give us a much nicer foundation for your graphical desktop.

Phase 4 — Switch networking carefully

Do not do this until your current Internet connection is confirmed working.

Install:

doas xbps-install -S NetworkManager

Make sure D-Bus is running:

sv status dbus

Add yourself to the network group:

doas usermod -aG network "$USER"

Now disable the standalone network services:

doas rm /var/service/wpa_supplicant

If dhcpcd is still running:

doas rm /var/service/dhcpcd

Then enable NetworkManager:

doas ln -s /etc/sv/NetworkManager /var/service/

Check:

sv status NetworkManager

Then:

nmcli device

and:

nmcli connection show

For Wi-Fi:

nmcli device wifi list

Then connect:

nmcli device wifi connect "YOUR_SSID" password "YOUR_PASSWORD"
BUT

Given the networking problems we had during installation, I want us to do this as a controlled migration, not blindly execute it.

If your current wpa_supplicant configuration is rock solid, we can even postpone this until after Mango is running.

Phase 5 — Seatd

We're sticking with this.

Install:

doas xbps-install -S seatd

Enable:

doas ln -s /etc/sv/seatd /var/service/

Add your user to _seatd, not seat:

doas usermod -aG _seatd "$USER"

The current Void handbook explicitly specifies _seatd for non-root access.

Check:

groups

after logging out and back in.

Phase 6 — Turnstile

This is the piece I think makes our original seatd decision considerably better.

Install:

doas xbps-install -S turnstile

Enable:

doas ln -s /etc/sv/turnstiled /var/service/

Then log out and back in.

Why?

Because seatd does not provide XDG_RUNTIME_DIR or a user session.

Turnstile can.

Void explicitly recommends:

turnstile + seatd + acpid

when using turnstile without elogind.

And turnstile can also manage per-user services, including a D-Bus session bus. That's very interesting for our eventual PipeWire/Mango setup.

So our architecture becomes:

                 login
                   │
               turnstile
             ┌─────┴─────┐
             │           │
       XDG_RUNTIME_DIR  user services
             │           │
             │          D-Bus
             │           │
             └─────┬─────┘
                   │
                 Mango

That's much cleaner than trying to make Mango itself carry all of this.

Phase 7 — SSD/TRIM

Yes. Absolutely.

This is one of the things we should configure.

First:

lsblk --discard

Void says non-zero DISC-GRAN and DISC-MAX indicate that the device supports discard/TRIM.

Then test:

doas fstrim /

If successful you'll see something like:

/: xxx GiB (.... bytes) trimmed
Periodic TRIM

I would not put discard into /etc/fstab by default.

Void actually recommends periodic TRIM and says continuous discard can be undesirable on some SSDs.

For our minimalist system, I'd use a weekly job.

We have two choices.

Option A — cron

Install a cron implementation and put:

#!/bin/sh

fstrim /

in:

/etc/cron.weekly/fstrim

Void documents exactly this approach.

Option B — Void's snooze

Void also provides snooze-weekly, which runs scripts in /etc/cron.weekly.

I'd actually investigate using snooze + the weekly directory for our setup before installing a full cron daemon.

That fits our philosophy nicely:

Need scheduled tasks?
       ↓
snooze
       ↓
run weekly scripts

instead of:

install a permanent cron daemon

We'll decide when we reach that step.

Phase 8 — Check swap

You told me earlier that this machine has 8 GB RAM.

We need to verify what we actually created.

Run:

swapon --show

and:

free -h

If you have swap already, great.

If you don't, we'll decide between:

swap partition

or

swapfile

before doing anything else.

Void recommends swap particularly for low-RAM machines and notes that hibernation requires swap.

For your system, I'm leaning toward a swapfile if one wasn't created during installation, unless you've got a specific reason to use a partition.

Phase 9 — Time and timezone

Since you're dual-booting Windows, this deserves attention.

Void normally uses:

hardware clock = UTC

but Windows normally uses local time. Void explicitly warns that this can cause conflicts in a dual-boot setup.

Check:

date

and:

date -u

Check:

ls -l /etc/localtime

For Kenya we'd normally use:

Africa/Nairobi

so:

doas ln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/Africa/Nairobi /etc/localtime

Then we'll configure an NTP client.

I'm leaning toward chrony:

doas xbps-install -S chrony

because Void describes Chrony as being designed for fast synchronization and accuracy under varying conditions.

Enable:

doas ln -s /etc/sv/chronyd /var/service/

We'll verify the exact service name on your installed package before enabling it.

Phase 10 — CPU microcode + firmware

This is another yes.

First determine your CPU:

lscpu | grep 'Vendor ID'

For Intel:

doas xbps-install -S intel-ucode

Void notes that Intel microcode comes from the nonfree repository and requires regenerating the initramfs after first installation.

For AMD, the relevant firmware package is:

linux-firmware-amd

which also contains AMD CPU microcode.

We'll do this based on your actual CPU rather than blindly installing both.

Phase 11 — GPU

Before Mango.

This is important.

We need:

lspci | grep -E 'VGA|3D|Display'

Then we'll install the appropriate graphics stack.

For example, AMD generally needs:

mesa-dri
linux-firmware-amd

and potentially Vulkan/VA-API packages. Void's AMD documentation specifies mesa-dri for OpenGL and mesa-vulkan-radeon for Vulkan.

Intel similarly needs:

mesa-dri
linux-firmware-intel

with the appropriate Vulkan/VA-API packages depending on what we want.

We shouldn't install random GPU packages until we know your GPU.

Phase 12 — Power management

Because you're on a laptop, this is worth doing.

Void's default installation may already have acpid. Its handbook says acpid handles ACPI events and can use zzz for suspend.

Check:

sv status acpid

If it isn't installed/running:

doas xbps-install -S acpid

and:

doas ln -s /etc/sv/acpid /var/service/
TLP?

Potentially:

doas xbps-install -S tlp

Void supports it for laptop power saving.

But I wouldn't install TLP yet.

First get Mango working.

Then measure battery/power behavior.

If we need TLP, add it.

Phase 13 — Firewall

This is another thing I'd consider, but not blindly enable yet.

Void supports both iptables and nftables and documents nftables as the modern replacement for the xtables family.

I'd eventually prefer:

nftables

over installing some giant firewall frontend.

But first:

ss -tulpn

Let's see what is actually listening.

If you're not running:

SSH server
web server
databases
etc.

your attack surface is already quite small.

Then we can make a simple nftables policy.

Phase 14 — AppArmor

This is optional, but interesting.

Void supports AppArmor. It can constrain applications using mandatory access-control policies.

I'd put it in our:

Security hardening — later

bucket.

Not in:

Essential post-install

because I don't want our first Mango boot buried under security policy debugging.

Phase 15 — Mango

Only after the foundation is healthy.

Install:

doas xbps-install -S mangowc foot rofi waybar mako swaybg wl-clipboard

Then:

mkdir -p ~/.config/mango

and we'll build the Mango configuration properly.

At this point:

D-Bus       ✅
Network     ✅
seatd       ✅
turnstile   ✅
GPU         ✅
firmware    ✅
TRIM        ✅
time        ✅
swap        ✅

Then:

                    TTY
                     │
                     ▼
                   Mango
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ▼          ▼          ▼
        foot        rofi      waybar

No display manager yet.

I still strongly prefer launching Mango manually initially.

Phase 16 — PipeWire

And yes — later.

Once Mango itself works:

doas xbps-install -S pipewire

Current Void's PipeWire package brings in WirePlumber as the session manager.

We'll make sure:

turnstile
    ↓
D-Bus session
    ↓
PipeWire
    ↓
WirePlumber

works properly.

This is especially important because we're choosing seatd rather than elogind. Void notes that without elogind, PipeWire needs appropriate device access, and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR must be configured.

So postponing PipeWire is actually a good thing.

Things I'm deliberately NOT putting into the initial setup

This is important.

We don't need:

❌ Display manager

No LightDM yet.

❌ Full desktop environment

No GNOME/KDE/XFCE.

❌ PulseAudio

We're going PipeWire.

❌ elogind

We're committed to seatd for now.

❌ TLP immediately

Only if we actually need it.

❌ AppArmor immediately

Security hardening comes after the base system is stable.

❌ Firewall immediately

First inspect what's listening.

❌ Flatpak

Only if you actually need software from it.

❌ Xorg
